<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richmond, Virginia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richmond is the capital of the US state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-river">James River</a>. It was the capital of the Confederate States of America from 1861 to 1865 and its main industrial centre, with the Tredegar Iron Works. Repeated Union campaigns aimed to take it, and its fall in April 1865 led directly to Lee's surrender at Appomattox.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richmond-virginia.jpg" alt="Richmond, Virginia" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bruce Emmerling · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_downtown_view_of_Richmond,_VA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Richmond stands at the fall line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-river">James River</a>, the rocky point that separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>'s Piedmont from the Tidewater and the highest point the river can be navigated. The Piedmont is a region of low, rolling hills lying between the flat Tidewater and the Blue Ridge Mountains. The site was an important village in the Powhatan Confederacy; the name of the Powhatan people is traced to Pawat-hanne, meaning falls in a stream, a reference to the falls of the James. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> was founded in April 1607, Captain Christopher Newport led explorers up the river to this inhabited area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>English colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> briefly settled the place from 1609 to 1611. Richmond was founded in 1737, with a street grid laid out between what are now Broad, 17th and 25th Streets and the river, and was incorporated in 1742.</p><p>In 1775 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-henry">Patrick Henry</a> delivered his speech ending 'Give me liberty or give me death!' at St. John's Church. The state capital moved there from Williamsburg on 18 April 1780, partly to give a more central position for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>'s growing western population. In 1781 Loyalist troops led by Benedict Arnold raided and burned the city, and Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> fled. Richmond recovered quickly. The Virginia Statute for Religious Freedom, drafted by Jefferson, was enacted in 1786, and the State Capitol, designed by Jefferson and Charles-Louis Clérisseau in the Greek Revival style, was completed in 1788.</p><p>The Virginia Convention voted to secede from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on 17 April 1861, five days after the attack on Fort Sumter, pending a popular vote. On 20 May the Confederate States Congress voted to move its capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>, to Richmond, and on 23 May Virginia voters ratified the Ordinance of Secession. After the war the city again became a major centre of production, with iron-front buildings and large brick factories, a railway crossroads with what is described as the world's first triple railway crossing, and a centre of tobacco processing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Richmond is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and has been an independent city since 1871; its population was 226,610 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. In the early twentieth century its Jackson Ward, a business district built by freed people and their descendants, was known as the Wall Street of Black America, and in 1903 Maggie L. Walker chartered the St. Luke Penny Savings Bank and became the first Black woman to serve as a bank president in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The city contains many buildings in Georgian, Federal and Greek Revival styles, among others, and has designated 45 historic districts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richmond, Virginia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Breton knights served widely abroad: a large Breton contingent fought for William of Normandy at the Battle of Hastings in 1066, and its leaders, such as Alan the Red, received great estates in England, including the honour of <b>Richmond</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Abraham Lincoln toured <b>Richmond, Virginia</b>, two days after its evacuation, greeted by crowds of newly freed Black residents.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A fire destroyed the Richmond Theatre in <b>Richmond, Virginia</b>, killing 72 people, among them Governor George William Smith and former senator Abraham B. Venable.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-26">26 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Confederate forces under Joseph E. Johnston and G.W. Smith attacked George B. McClellan's Union army at Seven Pines, outside the Confederate capital of <b>Richmond, Virginia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-31">31 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
